# Build steps whose work nothing runs automatically, and why that is correct.
#
# Enforced in both directions by the step-coverage gate in build.zig, which
# walks the real dependency graph rather than parsing source: a top-level step
# whose dependency closure is not reached by `zig build test`, by a step
# `scripts/verify.sh` or a CI workflow invokes, or by a shell gate one of those
# runs directly, must appear here. A row for a step that something now runs
# fails the same gate, and so does a row naming a step that no longer exists.
#
# One row per (step, reason slug). A row is a claim that this step is an
# operation a human asks for, not a check that was wired and forgotten. That
# distinction is the whole point: `scripts/test-examples.sh` was documented as
# outside `zig build test` and run only from verify.sh, so 56 example suites
# went unrun while `zig build test` reported a pass.
#
# The list only ratchets down. Adding a row is a deliberate widening.

# Manual run commands. `zig build run -- handler.ts` and `zig build cli -- ...`
# start a process for a human to interact with; there is no verdict to consume.
run interactive
cli interactive

# Runs the deterministic playbook server and blocks. `zig build test` already
# compiles the executable - deliberately, since nothing installs it and a break
# would otherwise surface only when somebody ran this by hand - so what is
# uncovered here is the server run, not the build.
zttp-standin interactive-server

# Measurements, not assertions. Both print numbers and pass regardless, so
# there is no failure for a gate to catch. The gated forms exist and do run:
# `bench-check` compares against benchmarks/perf-baseline.json in CI, and
# `test-compile-bench` runs the unit tests behind the compile benchmarks.
bench measurement
compile-bench measurement
production-branch-metric-json measurement

# Replaces the committed performance baseline and refuses dirty source. A
# release maintainer runs it explicitly after deciding to recalibrate.
bench-record release-operation

# Spends real model time against a live provider. `expert-qualification`
# consumes three report-only live runs and edits no default; running it on
# every build would bill every build.
expert-qualification manual-model-spend

# Needs a local MLX server, which this project never starts or manages. The
# absence from the aggregate step is stated at its definition in build.zig.
test-expert-mlx-e2e needs-local-server

# Needs Chrome and Node, which are not build dependencies of this repository.
# The step fails loudly when either is missing, which is why it stays explicit
# rather than being folded into a suite that would then fail on a clean machine.
test-zts-overview-browser needs-browser

# Emits a sealed training bundle and refuses a dirty tree, so it cannot run
# inside a build that is itself producing artifacts.
zts-training-export release-artifact

# Verifies a demo passport against a live artifact. `test-demo-passport-check`
# runs the unit tests behind it; this step is the operation.
demo-passport-check release-operation
